{
  "rationale": "These tests check fundamental properties any correct solution must satisfy: output is a non-negative integer; it is zero exactly when the condition already holds; it is positive otherwise. Lower and upper bounds are derived from the problem: the answer must be at least the gap between max(b) and min(a), and at most the cost of two simple feasible thresholds. Metamorphic relations exploit problem structure: shifting all values by a constant leaves the cost unchanged; duplicating arrays doubles the cost; reordering within each array does not affect the answer; and increasing an element of a (or decreasing an element of b) must not increase the cost. The single-element case is a precise check for minimal inputs. The first test to drop would be test_single_element_case, as it only applies when n=m=1 and is covered by other bounds in practice.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_non_negative_integer",
      "intent": "The output must be a non-negative integer. Parse the output string as an integer and assert it is >= 0. A negative output or non-integer string indicates a wrong answer."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_zero_if_already_satisfied",
      "intent": "If the minimum of array a is at least the maximum of array b, then no operations are needed and the output must be exactly 0. Compute min(a) and max(b) from the input; if min(a) >= max(b), assert the output equals 0. Any non-zero output violates the specification."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_positive_if_not_satisfied",
      "intent": "If the minimum of array a is less than the maximum of array b, then at least one operation is required, so the output must be positive. Compute min(a) and max(b); if min(a) < max(b), parse the output as an integer and assert it is > 0. A zero or negative output is wrong."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_gap",
      "intent": "The output must be at least the difference between max(b) and min(a) when that difference is positive, because closing that gap requires at least that many operations. Compute max(b) - min(a); if this is positive, assert the output integer is >= that value. An output smaller than this gap is impossible and indicates an error."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_upper_bound_feasible",
      "intent": "The output cannot exceed the cost of two simple feasible strategies: setting the threshold to max(b) (increasing all a below it) or to min(a) (decreasing all b above it). Compute cost1 = sum(max(0, max(b) - a_i) for a_i in a) and cost2 = sum(max(0, b_i - min(a)) for b_i in b). The output must be <= min(cost1, cost2). An output larger than this min is not minimal."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_shift_invariance",
      "intent": "Adding the same constant to every element of both arrays does not change the condition or the minimal cost, because the problem shifts uniformly. Create a modified input by adding a constant (e.g., 1000) to every element of a and b (ensuring they stay ≥1). Run the program on this shifted input and assert the output equals the original output. A difference indicates inconsistency."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_duplication",
      "intent": "If each array is concatenated with itself (doubling the size), the minimal cost should double because each element contributes independently. Create new arrays a' = a + a and b' = b + b. Run the program on this duplicated input and assert the output is exactly twice the original output. Any other ratio violates linearity of the cost."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_reordering",
      "intent": "The order of elements within each array does not affect the answer, as only multisets matter. Create a modified input by reversing the order of elements in a and in b. Run the program on this reordered input and assert the output is unchanged. A difference indicates an order dependency that should not exist."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_single_element_case",
      "intent": "When both arrays have exactly one element, the answer must be max(0, b[0] - a[0]), because either increase a to b or decrease b to a. If n=1 and m=1, parse the output as an integer and assert it equals max(0, b[0] - a[0]). Any other value is wrong for this special case."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonicity",
      "intent": "Increasing any element of a (or decreasing any element of b) should not increase the minimal cost, because the condition becomes easier to satisfy. Choose an index i where a[i] < 10^9 and increase a[i] by 1 to create a modified input. Run the program on both original and modified inputs and assert the output for the modified input is <= the original output. If no a[i] < 10^9, choose a b[j] > 1 and decrease it by 1 instead. If neither modification is possible, skip the assertion. An increase in cost violates monotonicity."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "The tests combine full verification (certificate) with sanity checks and metamorphic relations to catch backdoors and other errors. The full certificate computes compatibility and free positions directly from the specification, ensuring any deviation is caught. Boundedness and mod26 are basic invariants. The m=0 test handles a trivial case. Two metamorphic relations check that adding a free position multiplies the answer by 26. Divisibility tests ensure the exponent of 26 is at least the free positions at ends and in gaps. The compatibility-zero test specifically targets inconsistent overlaps. The non‑overlap special case provides an additional check for a common scenario. If one test had to be dropped, it would be test_mod26 as it is the weakest and subsumed by the certificate.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_bounded",
      "intent": "Assert that the returned answer is between 0 and 26^n modulo 10^9+7 inclusive, because the count of strings cannot exceed the total number of strings of length n (26^n) and cannot be negative."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_mod26",
      "intent": "Assert that the answer modulo 26 is either 0 or 1, since any power of 26 (≥1) is divisible by 26, and 26^0 = 1; the answer 0 also satisfies 0 mod 26 = 0."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_m_zero",
      "intent": "If m = 0 (no occurrences written), assert that the answer equals 26^n modulo 10^9+7, because every position is free."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_compatibility_zero",
      "intent": "Check pairwise compatibility of the occurrences: for each consecutive y_i, if they overlap (y[i+1] ≤ y[i] + |p| - 1), verify that the prefix of p of length |p| - (y[i+1] - y[i]) equals the suffix of the same length. If any such check fails, assert that the answer must be 0."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_free_ends_divisibility",
      "intent": "If the answer is non‑zero, compute the number of free positions before the first occurrence (y[0]-1) and after the last occurrence (n - (y[-1]+|p|-1)). Assert that the answer is divisible by 26 raised to each of these counts modulo 10^9+7, because those positions are unconditionally free."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_gap_divisibility",
      "intent": "If the answer is non‑zero, for each consecutive pair of y_i that do not overlap (y[i+1] ≥ y[i] + |p|), compute the gap length = y[i+1] - (y[i] + |p|). Assert that the answer is divisible by 26 raised to the gap length modulo 10^9+7, because each gap consists of free positions."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_metamorphic_inc_n",
      "intent": "Create a new valid input by increasing n by 1 while keeping p and y unchanged. Run the program on this input to get answer2. Assert that answer2 equals (original_answer * 26) modulo 10^9+7, because adding one free position at the end multiplies the count by 26."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_metamorphic_shift_right",
      "intent": "Create a new valid input by increasing n by 1 and increasing each y_i by 1. Run the program on this input to get answer2. Assert that answer2 equals (original_answer * 26) modulo 10^9+7, because shifting all occurrences right by one adds a free position at the beginning."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_no_overlap_special",
      "intent": "If all consecutive y_i are non‑overlapping (y[i+1] ≥ y[i] + |p| for all i), compute free = n - m * |p|. Assert that the answer equals 26^free modulo 10^9+7, because the forced positions are exactly m disjoint blocks of length |p|."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_full_certificate",
      "intent": "Compute whether the placements are compatible as in test_compatibility_zero. If incompatible, assert answer = 0. If compatible, compute the union of intervals [y_i, y_i+|p|-1] to get the total number of forced positions, then free = n - forced. Assert that the answer equals 26^free modulo 10^9+7, which is the exact count from the specification."
    }
  ]
}

