    },
    {
      "name": "test_all_strings_of_same_length",
      "intent": "For the integer n in the test case, generate every possible string of length n over 'L' and 'R'. For each such string, run the program on a test case with that string and compare its output to the brute-force minimum for that string. If any output is wrong, the test fails. This ensures correctness on all strings of this length."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_rotation_invariance",
      "intent": "Construct a second test case by cyclically shifting the original string by one position (first character becomes last). Run the program on both the original and shifted inputs and assert the outputs are equal. A violation is differing answers, breaking the rotational symmetry of the circle."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_complement_reversal_invariance",
      "intent": "Construct a second test case by reversing the original string and swapping all 'L' and 'R' (complement). Run the program on both the original and transformed inputs and assert the outputs are equal. This checks invariance under reflection of the circle, which must hold."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_bounds",
      "intent": "Parse the output as an integer and verify it is between 0 and n inclusive. A violation is an output outside this range, which is impossible because at most n players can be changed and at least 0."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_integer",
      "intent": "Verify that the output is a valid integer (parses as an int without error). A violation is a non-integer output, contradicting the problem statement."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_logical_zero",
      "intent": "Check whether the original configuration is already logical (each player attacked by exactly one neighbor attacks that neighbor). If logical, assert output is 0; if not, assert output is at least 1. A violation is a logical configuration with non-zero output or a non-logical configuration with output 0."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_disjoint_violations",
      "intent": "Identify all players that are forced (attacked by exactly one neighbor) but are not attacking that attacker. Greedily select a set of such violations where the involved player sets {i-1,i,i+1} are disjoint. Let L be the size of this set. Assert the output is at least L. A violation is an output smaller than L, which cannot fix all disjoint violations."
