{
  "rationale": "These tests check basic bounds, special cases, and consistency relations derived from the specification. Bounds ensure the answer lies within feasible ranges. The power check verifies that exact denomination inputs yield the minimal answer of 1. Metamorphic relations catch errors in incremental changes by comparing with outputs for nearby inputs. The DP recurrence is a strong necessary condition for optimality, checking that the output satisfies the Bellman equation. The feasible split test verifies that the output is achievable by some combination of powers of 6 and 9. If one test were to be dropped first, test_metamorphic_plus6 would be chosen, as it is a special case of the more general DP recurrence test.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_bounds",
      "intent": "Assert that the output integer k satisfies 1 ≤ k ≤ N. A violation is an output outside this range, which would be impossible since at least one operation is needed and at most N operations (using 1-yen coins) suffice."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_upper_bound_base6",
      "intent": "Compute the sum of digits of N in base 6, which is a valid number of operations using only powers of 6. The output k must not exceed this sum. A violation is k larger than this digit sum, meaning the output claims more operations than a known feasible solution."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_upper_bound_base9",
      "intent": "Compute the sum of digits of N in base 9, a valid number of operations using only powers of 9. The output k must not exceed this sum. A violation is k larger than this digit sum."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_max_coin",
      "intent": "Find the largest denomination d (a power of 6 or 9, or 1) that is ≤ N. Since each operation withdraws at most d, k must be at least ceil(N/d). A violation is k smaller than this ceiling, which would imply withdrawing more than d per operation on average."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_power_check",
      "intent": "If N equals 1 or is a power of 6 (6^k, k≥1) or a power of 9 (9^k, k≥1), then one operation suffices. The output must be exactly 1. A violation is any output other than 1 for such N."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_metamorphic_plus1",
      "intent": "For N > 1, run the program on input N-1 to obtain output k1. Since we can add a 1-yen operation to a solution for N-1, k must be ≤ k1 + 1. A violation is k > k1 + 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_metamorphic_plus6",
      "intent": "For N ≥ 7, run the program on input N-6 to obtain output k6. Adding a 6-yen operation to a solution for N-6 gives a valid solution for N, so k must be ≤ k6 + 1. A violation is k > k6 + 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_metamorphic_plus9",
      "intent": "For N ≥ 10, run the program on input N-9 to obtain output k9. Adding a 9-yen operation to a solution for N-9 gives a valid solution for N, so k must be ≤ k9 + 1. A violation is k > k9 + 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_dp_recurrence",
      "intent": "If N is a denomination (1 or a power of 6 or 9), assert output is 1. Otherwise, compute all denominations d < N (1, powers of 6, powers of 9). For each d, run the program on N-d to get output k_d. Let m be the minimum of these k_d. By the optimal substructure, the output for N must equal 1 + m. A violation is any deviation from this equality."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_feasible_split",
      "intent": "Iterate i from 0 to N, compute the digit sum of i in base 6 and of N-i in base 9. If for some i the sum equals the output k, the test passes. If no such i exists, it is a violation because k would not correspond to any feasible split into powers of 6 and 9."
    }
  ]
}

