{
  "rationale": "The tests are designed to catch backdoors by verifying properties that every correct solution must satisfy, based solely on the problem specification. They include symmetry (permutation invariance), linearity under translation, bounds derived from order statistics (expected min/max and possible min/max), exact verification for small instances, special cases (n=2, deterministic bids), and basic sanity checks. These properties are directly implied by the definition of expected second-price payment and the independence and uniformity of bids. If a test must be dropped, test_finite is the least informative as it only checks output format.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_permutation",
      "intent": "The expected payment must be invariant under any permutation of the input lines because the companies are indistinguishable except for their bid ranges. Permute the lines, run the program on both the original and permuted inputs, and assert the outputs are equal within an absolute tolerance of 1e-9."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_translation",
      "intent": "If all bid ranges can be shifted upward by a positive integer d without exceeding 10000 (i.e., max(R_i) ≤ 10000 - d), then the expected payment should increase by exactly d. For d=1 (or the largest safe d), construct a new input by adding d to each L_i and R_i. Run the program on both inputs and assert that output2 - output1 = d within 1e-9 absolute tolerance."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_expected_min",
      "intent": "The expected payment cannot be less than the expected minimum bid. Compute E_min = sum_{x=1}^{10000} ∏_i P(bid_i ≥ x), where P(bid_i ≥ x) is 1 if x ≤ L_i, 0 if x > R_i, and (R_i - x + 1)/(R_i - L_i + 1) otherwise. Assert the program's output is at least E_min - 1e-9."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_upper_bound_expected_max",
      "intent": "The expected payment cannot exceed the expected maximum bid. Compute E_max = sum_{x=1}^{10000} (1 - ∏_i (1 - P(bid_i ≥ x))), with P(bid_i ≥ x) as above. Assert the program's output is at most E_max + 1e-9."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_n2_equals_expected_min",
      "intent": "When there are exactly two companies, the payment equals the minimum of their bids. Therefore, the output must equal the expected minimum of the two bids. Compute E_min as in test_lower_bound_expected_min (using only the two companies) and assert equality within 1e-9."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_small_exact",
      "intent": "If the total number of possible bid combinations (∏_i (R_i - L_i + 1)) is at most 10^6, compute the exact expected payment by enumerating all combinations, calculating the second highest bid for each, and averaging. Assert the program's output matches this exact value within 1e-9."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_possible",
      "intent": "The expected payment must be at least the minimum possible second highest bid. Compute the minimum possible second highest as min_i (max_{j ≠ i} L_j). Assert the output is at least this value minus 1e-9."
    },
