      "name": "test_remove",
      "intent": "Removing a bidder cannot increase the expected second-highest. If n > 2, create a reduced input by removing the last bidder. Run the program on both and assert that the original output is at least the reduced output minus 1e-9."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_n2",
      "intent": "For exactly two bidders, the second-highest bid is the minimum of the two bids. If n == 2, compute E[min(X_1,X_2)] by summing over x from 1 to 10000 the product P(X_1 >= x) * P(X_2 >= x). Assert that the program's output equals this expectation within 1e-9."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "I implemented each intent from the plan as a property test based solely on the specification. I strengthened some: test_certificate uses the sum-of-probabilities formula which the spec implies (second-price expectation equals sum of probabilities that at least two bids ≥ x). I weakened others: test_shift checks a shift by c=1 only if safe (max R_i +1 ≤ 10000), else skips. test_lower_bound_pairwise and test_upper_bound_max compute bounds using the given input's ranges. All tests are property-based: they verify relations the specification forces, without computing the expected value directly. They should hold for any correct solution. If I had to drop one, I'd drop test_possible_bounds as it's the weakest (just an interval bound).",
