{
  "rationale": "The tests combine boundedness, invariants, certificates, and metamorphic relations to catch deviations from the specification. The general certificate (test_general_certificate) directly computes the correct answer via greedy leftmost and rightmost subsequence matches, catching any wrong answer. Metamorphic reversal and addition of irrelevant characters ensure symmetry and invariance properties implied by the problem. Boundedness and invariant tests catch gross errors like counts exceeding possible splits or violating necessary conditions. The exhaustive check for small inputs provides an independent verification. If one test were dropped, test_small_exhaustive would be the least essential because it is redundant with test_general_certificate for small inputs.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_bound",
      "intent": "The answer must be between 0 and max(0, len(t) - 2*len(s) + 1), because each part needs at least len(s) characters. A violation is an answer outside this interval."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_not_subseq",
      "intent": "If s is not a subsequence of t (checked by greedy matching), then no split can make both parts contain s, so the answer must be 0. A non-zero answer in this case is wrong."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_length_bound",
      "intent": "If len(t) < 2*len(s), then at least one part would have fewer than len(s) characters and cannot contain s as a subsequence, so the answer must be 0. Any positive answer here is invalid."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_single_char",
      "intent": "When s has length 1, the answer equals (last index of that character in t minus first index) if the character appears, else 0. A mismatch indicates an error."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_general_certificate",
      "intent": "Compute L as the smallest index where s becomes a subsequence of t[0:L+1] (leftmost greedy match end), and R as the largest index where s becomes a subsequence of t[R:] (rightmost greedy match start). The answer must equal max(0, R - L). Any deviation is incorrect."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_reversal",
      "intent": "Reverse both s and t to get s_rev and t_rev. Running the program on (s_rev, t_rev) must yield the same answer as on the original input, because the problem is symmetric under reversal. Different answers signal a bug."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_prepend",
      "intent": "If there exists a lowercase letter not in s, prepend it to t to form t2. The answer for (s, t2) must equal the original answer, because adding an irrelevant character at the start does not affect valid splits. A change indicates an error."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_append",
      "intent": "If there exists a lowercase letter not in s, append it to t to form t2. The answer for (s, t2) must equal the original answer, as adding an irrelevant character at the end preserves valid splits. A difference is wrong."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_both_ends",
      "intent": "If there exists a lowercase letter not in s, prepend and append it to t to form t2. The answer for (s, t2) must equal the original answer, because adding irrelevant characters at both ends does not change the count. Any discrepancy is invalid."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_small_exhaustive",
      "intent": "If len(t) <= 100, compute the answer by brute force: for each split point i, check if s is a subsequence of both parts. The returned answer must match this count. A mismatch catches errors on small inputs."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "The solution uses an AVL tree to manage indices of the k largest values. A bug in rotation may corrupt order statistics. Tests include exhaustive verification (small inputs), necessary conditions (output in a, bounded by k-th largest), and metamorphic relations (scaling/shifting) that hold because optimal subsequence depends only on relative values. Subsequence consistency ensures outputs form a valid subsequence; sum maximum and multiset subset check optimality properties. These together catch miscalculations from tree errors.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_exhaustive",
      "intent": "For each query, enumerate all subsets of size k, find those with maximum sum, among those pick lexicographically smallest by values, and verify the pos-th element equals the output."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_values_from_a",
      "intent": "Every output value must appear in the input array a; assert each output is an element of a."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_bounds",
      "intent": "Each output value must lie between the minimum and maximum of a (inclusive)."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_scaling",
      "intent": "If all a_i * 2 ≤ 10^9, create a modified input by doubling each a_i. Run both original and modified; assert each output on modified equals twice the corresponding original output."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_shifting",
      "intent": "If all a_i + 1 ≤ 10^9, create a modified input by adding 1 to each a_i. Run both; assert each output on modified equals the original output plus 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_subsequence_consistency",
      "intent": "For each distinct k, collect its queries sorted by pos. Verify that there exists an increasing sequence of indices in a whose values match these outputs in order (greedy leftmost matching)."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_sum_maximum",
      "intent": "For each k where queries cover all positions 1..k, sum the output values for that k. Compute the maximum possible sum for any subsequence of length k (by sorting a descending and summing the first k). Assert equality."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_multiset_subset",
      "intent": "For each distinct k, compute the multiset of the k largest values (sort a descending, take first k). Verify each output for that k belongs to this multiset and its count in outputs does not exceed its count in the multiset."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_standard_algorithm",
      "intent": "Compute the optimal subsequence for each k: sort pairs (value, index) by value descending then index ascending, take first k, sort these by index ascending. For each query, assert the pos-th element of this sequence equals the output."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_bounded_by_kth_largest",
      "intent": "For each query, compute the k-th largest value in a (sort descending, take the k-th). Assert the output is ≥ that value and ≤ max(a)."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "The submitted solution may contain a backdoor in tie‑breaking for duplicate values when n ≥ 10. A brute‑force verification on the small given inputs catches any outright error. Metamorphic tests (adding a constant, scaling, nesting) rely on specification‑guaranteed properties: adding a constant shifts outputs equally, scaling multiplies them proportionally, and the optimal subsequence for k is a subsequence of that for k+1. Simple invariants ensure outputs belong to the array and lie within its min‑max. For distinct and constant arrays, simpler certificates apply. Per‑query bounds check that each output is at least the k‑th largest value, and a sum check verifies the maximum‑sum property. If forced to drop one, the nesting test would be first, as it is covered by brute‑force on these small inputs.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_brute_force",
      "intent": "For each query (k, pos) in the input, enumerate all length‑k subsequences of the array, select those with maximum sum, and among those pick the lexicographically minimal sequence. Verify that the program's output equals the element at position pos in that sequence. A mismatch indicates a wrong answer."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_add_constant",
      "intent": "Create a modified input by adding 1 to every element of the array. Run the program on both original and modified inputs. For each query, the output for the modified input must be exactly 1 more than the output for the original input. A deviation violates the specification because adding a constant does not change the chosen indices, only their values."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_scaling",
      "intent": "Create a modified input by multiplying every element of the array by 2. Run the program on both original and modified inputs. For each query, the output for the modified input must be exactly twice the output for the original input. Scaling preserves the order of sums and lexicographic order, so the same indices are chosen."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_nesting",
      "intent": "For the given array, let k = min(n//2, 5). Construct a new input that queries all positions 1..k and 1..k+1. Run the program on this input. The sequence for k must be a subsequence of the sequence for k+1 (element‑wise in order). This holds because the set of indices for k is a subset of those for k+1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_output_in_array",
      "intent": "For each query output, verify that the value appears in the original array. The optimal subsequence consists solely of elements from the array, so any output not in the array is invalid."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_output_bounds",
      "intent": "For each query output, verify that it lies between the minimum and maximum values of the array (inclusive). This is a basic bound derived from the fact that the subsequence picks elements from the array."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_distinct_array",
      "intent": "If all elements of the array are distinct, compute for each query (k, pos) the optimal subsequence by taking the k largest values and sorting their indices ascending. The output must equal the value at position pos in that sequence. A mismatch indicates an error."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_constant_array",
      "intent": "If all elements of the array are equal, then for each query (k, pos) the optimal subsequence is the first k elements of the array (earliest indices). Thus the output must equal a[pos] (1‑indexed). Any other value is wrong."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_per_query_lower_bound",
      "intent": "For each query (k, pos), compute the k‑th largest value in the array (by sorting descending and taking the k‑th element). The output must be at least this value, because the optimal subsequence consists of the k largest values, each of which is at least as large as the smallest among them. Also, the output must be at most the maximum of the array. Violation indicates an incorrect selection."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_sum_maximum",
      "intent": "For each distinct k that appears in the original queries, construct a new input that queries all positions 1..k for that k. Run the program on this input, sum the outputs, and compare with the sum of the k largest values in the array. They must be equal, as the optimal subsequence must have the maximum possible sum. A difference reveals a wrong answer."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "These tests combine strong certificates (cumulative sum and simulation) that directly verify the output against the input, basic invariants (non‑negativity, bounds, length) that catch obvious mistakes, and metamorphic relations (appending a day with exactly m names, adding m to each a_i, scaling both a_i and m by 2) that probe edge cases like exact multiples. The cumulative and simulation tests are sufficient for correctness; the bounds tests guard against off‑by‑one errors; the metamorphic tests detect mis‑handling of carry‑over and scaling. If one test had to be dropped, test_upper_bound_ceil would be the first, as it is implied by the simulation test and offers only a quick sanity check.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_cumulative",
      "intent": "For each prefix i, the sum of the first i output numbers must equal the floor of the sum of the first i a_i divided by m. Parse the output into a list of integers t. Compute prefix sums of a and t, and assert that for every i, sum(t[0:i]) == prefix_sum_a_i // m."
    },
    {
