{
  "rationale": "Each test implements one numbered intent from the plan, using only the specification and the given input list. Strengths/weaknesses: (1) output format is exactly 'Yes\n' or 'No\n'; (2) stripped output must be 'Yes' or 'No'; (3) certificate check uses D ≤ T*S for Yes, D > T*S for No; (4) symmetry of swapping T and S (since time×speed is commutative); (5-8) monotonicity: increasing T or S cannot turn Yes into No, decreasing cannot turn No into Yes; (9) scaling D and T proportionally (if allowed within constraints) should preserve answer because condition D/(S) ≤ T scales linearly; (10) boundary equality: if D = T*S, answer must be Yes. All assertions derive from the specification's inequality D ≤ T*S. If dropping one, test_scaling_DT is most complex and depends on divisibility conditions.",
  "tests": [
    {
      "name": "test_output_format",
      "source": "def test_output_format(run, x):
